            "heading": "Practice Questions",
            "headingHinglish": "Practice Questions",
            "body": "1. Mr. Bhatt's haath is 48 cm and Tisha's haath is 42 cm. If both measure 10 haath of cloth, find the length each gets. What is the difference?\n\n2. Write the number and the unit in each statement:\n   (a) The length of the room is 4 m.\n   (b) The mass of the bag is 6 kg.\n   (c) The race took 15 s.\n   (d) The temperature of the water is 60 °C.\n\n3. Which of these statements is missing a unit? Rewrite it correctly by choosing a sensible unit.\n   (a) The pencil is 18 cm long.\n   (b) The baby weighs 7.\n   (c) The film lasts 2 hours.\n\n4. Sort these into physical quantities and non-physical ones: length of a road, loudness of someone's laughter (as \"funny\"), time taken to cook rice, the beauty of a rangoli, mass of a mango, temperature of tea, how much you miss your friend.\n\n5. A student says, \"The cloth is 5 haath long, so the length is fully described.\" Explain why this is not a good measurement, using the example of two people with different haath sizes.\n\n6. State True or False:\n   (a) A measurement needs both a number and a unit.\n   (b) \"The tea is hot\" is a measurement.\n   (c) Length is a physical quantity.\n   (d) Two persons using \"haath\" will always get the same length of cloth.\n\n7. Why was a common standard unit needed when people traded cloth and grain? Answer in 2-3 lines.\n\n### Answers\n\n1. Mr. Bhatt: 10 × 48 cm = **480 cm**. Tisha: 10 × 42 cm = **420 cm**. Farak = 480 cm − 420 cm = **60 cm**.\n   Jaanch: 420 cm + 60 cm = 480 cm ✅\n\n2. (a) Number **4**, unit **m**. (b) Number **6**, unit **kg**. (c) Number **15**, unit **s**. (d) Number **60**, unit **°C**.\n\n3. (b) mein unit gayab hai. \"The baby weighs 7\" mein pata nahi 7 kya hai. Sahi: \"The baby weighs **7 kg**.\" (a) mein 18 cm aur (c) mein 2 hours (time ki unit) theek hain.\n\n4. **Physical quantities:** length of a road, time taken to cook rice, mass of a mango, temperature of tea.\n   **Non-physical:** loudness of laughter as \"funny\" (ye raay hai), beauty of a rangoli, how much you miss your friend.\n\n5. \"5 haath\" mein number to hai par unit ki lambai har insaan ki alag hai. Mr. Bhatt ke 5 haath 240 cm, aur Tisha ke 5 haath 210 cm. Farak 30 cm ka hai. Isliye lambai poori tarah describe nahi hui. Standard unit jaise cm mein likhna hoga.\n\n6. (a) **True** ✅. (b) **False** ❌. Isme temperature ka number aur unit nahi hai, ye bas observation hai. (c) **True** ✅. (d) **False** ❌. Har insaan ka haath alag hai, to length alag aayegi (jaise 240 cm aur 210 cm).\n\n7. Jab log kapda ya anaaj bechte-kharidte the, to har insaan ki apni unit hoti thi (haath, muththi), jo alag-alag size ki thi. Isse jhagde aur nuksaan hote the. Ek common standard unit se sabko ek jaisa naap milta hai aur bharosa banta hai.\n\n---\n\nBahut accha. Ab aap jaante ho ki naapne ke liye number ke saath unit kyun zaroori hai, aur standard unit ki zaroorat kyun padi. Jab bhi koi answer likho, number ke baad unit zaroor likhna.\n\n**Aage kya?** Ab dekhte hain ki poori duniya ne kaunsa ek system chuna.",
            "bodyHinglish": "1. Mr. Bhatt's haath is 48 cm and Tisha's haath is 42 cm. If both measure 10 haath of cloth, find the length each gets. What is the difference?\n\n2. Write the number and the unit in each statement:\n   (a) The length of the room is 4 m.\n   (b) The mass of the bag is 6 kg.\n   (c) The race took 15 s.\n   (d) The temperature of the water is 60 °C.\n\n3. Which of these statements is missing a unit? Rewrite it correctly by choosing a sensible unit.\n   (a) The pencil is 18 cm long.\n   (b) The baby weighs 7.\n   (c) The film lasts 2 hours.\n\n4. Sort these into physical quantities and non-physical ones: length of a road, loudness of someone's laughter (as \"funny\"), time taken to cook rice, the beauty of a rangoli, mass of a mango, temperature of tea, how much you miss your friend.\n\n5. A student says, \"The cloth is 5 haath long, so the length is fully described.\" Explain why this is not a good measurement, using the example of two people with different haath sizes.\n\n6. State True or False:\n   (a) A measurement needs both a number and a unit.\n   (b) \"The tea is hot\" is a measurement.\n   (c) Length is a physical quantity.\n   (d) Two persons using \"haath\" will always get the same length of cloth.\n\n7. Why was a common standard unit needed when people traded cloth and grain? Answer in 2-3 lines.\n\n### Answers\n\n1. Mr. Bhatt: 10 × 48 cm = **480 cm**. Tisha: 10 × 42 cm = **420 cm**. Farak = 480 cm − 420 cm = **60 cm**.\n   Jaanch: 420 cm + 60 cm = 480 cm ✅\n\n2. (a) Number **4**, unit **m**. (b) Number **6**, unit **kg**. (c) Number **15**, unit **s**. (d) Number **60**, unit **°C**.\n\n3. (b) mein unit gayab hai. \"The baby weighs 7\" mein pata nahi 7 kya hai. Sahi: \"The baby weighs **7 kg**.\" (a) mein 18 cm aur (c) mein 2 hours (time ki unit) theek hain.\n\n4. **Physical quantities:** length of a road, time taken to cook rice, mass of a mango, temperature of tea.\n   **Non-physical:** loudness of laughter as \"funny\" (ye raay hai), beauty of a rangoli, how much you miss your friend.\n\n5. \"5 haath\" mein number to hai par unit ki lambai har insaan ki alag hai. Mr. Bhatt ke 5 haath 240 cm, aur Tisha ke 5 haath 210 cm. Farak 30 cm ka hai. Isliye lambai poori tarah describe nahi hui. Standard unit jaise cm mein likhna hoga.\n\n6. (a) **True** ✅. (b) **False** ❌. Isme temperature ka number aur unit nahi hai, ye bas observation hai. (c) **True** ✅. (d) **False** ❌. Har insaan ka haath alag hai, to length alag aayegi (jaise 240 cm aur 210 cm).\n\n7. Jab log kapda ya anaaj bechte-kharidte the, to har insaan ki apni unit hoti thi (haath, muththi), jo alag-alag size ki thi. Isse jhagde aur nuksaan hote the. Ek common standard unit se sabko ek jaisa naap milta hai aur bharosa banta hai.\n\n---\n\nBahut accha. Ab aap jaante ho ki naapne ke liye number ke saath unit kyun zaroori hai, aur standard unit ki zaroorat kyun padi. Jab bhi koi answer likho, number ke baad unit zaroor likhna.\n\n**Aage kya?** Ab dekhte hain ki poori duniya ne kaunsa ek system chuna."
