          {
            "heading": "Part F: Gravity aur Newton ke laws",
            "headingHinglish": "Part F: Gravity aur Newton ke laws",
            "body": "Pichhle chapter ka sabak yahan kaam aata hai. Dekhte hain aam ke saath kya hua:\n\n- Daal par laga aam **rest** mein tha. Daal use pakde thi (daal ka force upar, gravity ka force neeche: balanced).\n- Jab aam toota, daal ka force hat gaya. Ab sirf gravity bacha: **unbalanced force**.\n- **Newton ka Doosra Law** kehta hai: unbalanced force lage to cheez **accelerate** karti hai. Aam ki speed 0 se badhti gayi: wo neeche ki taraf **accelerate** hua.\n- **Newton ka Pehla Law** kehta hai: agar koi unbalanced force na ho, to cheez ki motion nahi badalti. Isliye daal par aam tab tak ruka raha jab tak force balanced tha.\n\nSamajhne ke liye ek chhota hypothetical (kalpanik) number: agar kisi 0.5 kg ki cheez par sirf 2 N ka unbalanced force lage, to a = F ÷ m = 2 N ÷ 0.5 kg = **4 m/s²**. Gravity ka force kis tarah ka acceleration deta hai, ye Topic 3 mein aayega. Abhi bas ye yaad rakho: gravity ek asli force hai, aur wahi niyam is par bhi lagte hain.\n\n**Jaanch:** 4 m/s² × 0.5 kg = 2 N ✅\n\n### Latakti cheez: jab kuch nahi girta\n\nEk bulb ko chhat se dhaage se latka diya. Bulb **rest** mein hai, gir nahi raha. Kya gravity band ho gayi? Nahi.\n\n```\n        ===== chhat =====\n              |\n              |   <- dhaage ka force (tension), upar\n              |\n            ( O )  bulb\n              |\n              v   <- gravity ka force, neeche\n```\n\n> **Figure 1.2** – Latke hue bulb par do force: gravity neeche, dhaage ka tension upar. Dono barabar aur ulte hain, isliye bulb rest mein hai.\n\nBulb par gravity neeche kheench rahi hai, aur dhaaga use upar kheench raha hai. Ye dono force **balanced** hain, isliye net force zero hai aur bulb rukha hua hai. (Is neeche ke force ko hum \"bulb ka **weight**\" kehte hain: iska poora hisaab Topic 5 mein.)",
            "bodyHinglish": "Pichhle chapter ka sabak yahan kaam aata hai. Dekhte hain aam ke saath kya hua:\n\n- Daal par laga aam **rest** mein tha. Daal use pakde thi (daal ka force upar, gravity ka force neeche: balanced).\n- Jab aam toota, daal ka force hat gaya. Ab sirf gravity bacha: **unbalanced force**.\n- **Newton ka Doosra Law** kehta hai: unbalanced force lage to cheez **accelerate** karti hai. Aam ki speed 0 se badhti gayi: wo neeche ki taraf **accelerate** hua.\n- **Newton ka Pehla Law** kehta hai: agar koi unbalanced force na ho, to cheez ki motion nahi badalti. Isliye daal par aam tab tak ruka raha jab tak force balanced tha.\n\nSamajhne ke liye ek chhota hypothetical (kalpanik) number: agar kisi 0.5 kg ki cheez par sirf 2 N ka unbalanced force lage, to a = F ÷ m = 2 N ÷ 0.5 kg = **4 m/s²**. Gravity ka force kis tarah ka acceleration deta hai, ye Topic 3 mein aayega. Abhi bas ye yaad rakho: gravity ek asli force hai, aur wahi niyam is par bhi lagte hain.\n\n**Jaanch:** 4 m/s² × 0.5 kg = 2 N ✅\n\n### Latakti cheez: jab kuch nahi girta\n\nEk bulb ko chhat se dhaage se latka diya. Bulb **rest** mein hai, gir nahi raha. Kya gravity band ho gayi? Nahi.\n\n```\n        ===== chhat =====\n              |\n              |   <- dhaage ka force (tension), upar\n              |\n            ( O )  bulb\n              |\n              v   <- gravity ka force, neeche\n```\n\n> **Figure 1.2** – Latke hue bulb par do force: gravity neeche, dhaage ka tension upar. Dono barabar aur ulte hain, isliye bulb rest mein hai.\n\nBulb par gravity neeche kheench rahi hai, aur dhaaga use upar kheench raha hai. Ye dono force **balanced** hain, isliye net force zero hai aur bulb rukha hua hai. (Is neeche ke force ko hum \"bulb ka **weight**\" kehte hain: iska poora hisaab Topic 5 mein.)"
          },
          {
            "heading": "Part G: Agar gravity na hoti to? (soch ka experiment)",
            "headingHinglish": "Part G: Agar gravity na hoti to? (soch ka experiment)",
            "body": "Mr. Yadav ne kaha, \"Maan le ek second ke liye gravity band ho jaaye.\"\n\n- Aam daal se toot kar neeche nahi girta, **seedha tairta hua hawa mein chala jata**.\n- Barish ki boondein neeche nahi aati.\n- Hum zameen par tik kar chal nahi paate, halka sa kudne par hi upar uthte chale jaate.\n- Dharti ki hawa (atmosphere) bhi bikhar kar chali jaati.\n\nEk jhalak: **antariksh yatri (astronaut)** space station mein hawa mein tairte dikhte hain. Kya wahan gravity nahi hoti? Ye ek aam galatfehmi hai, jiska sahi jawab hum **weightlessness** ke naam se Topic 5 aur 6 mein dekhenge.",
            "bodyHinglish": "Mr. Yadav ne kaha, \"Maan le ek second ke liye gravity band ho jaaye.\"\n\n- Aam daal se toot kar neeche nahi girta, **seedha tairta hua hawa mein chala jata**.\n- Barish ki boondein neeche nahi aati.\n- Hum zameen par tik kar chal nahi paate, halka sa kudne par hi upar uthte chale jaate.\n- Dharti ki hawa (atmosphere) bhi bikhar kar chali jaati.\n\nEk jhalak: **antariksh yatri (astronaut)** space station mein hawa mein tairte dikhte hain. Kya wahan gravity nahi hoti? Ye ek aam galatfehmi hai, jiska sahi jawab hum **weightlessness** ke naam se Topic 5 aur 6 mein dekhenge."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Part H: Newton aur seb ki kahani (kaha jaata hai)",
            "headingHinglish": "Part H: Newton aur seb ki kahani (kaha jaata hai)",
            "body": "**Kaha jaata hai** ki ek din Isaac Newton ek seb ke ped ke neeche baithe the, aur ek seb neeche gira. Is se Newton ke dimaag mein ek sawal aaya: \"Seb neeche kyun gira? Kya wahi force jo seb ko neeche kheench raha hai, chaand tak bhi pahunchta hai?\"\n\nYe kahani ek **legend (kimvadanti)** hai: kahani ka kitna hissa sach hai, ye pakka nahi kaha ja sakta. Par sachchi baat ye hai ki Newton ne ek bahut bada vichar socha: **jo force seb ko zameen par girata hai, wahi force chaand ko Dharti ke chakkar lagwata hai.** Yaani aasman aur zameen ke liye alag-alag niyam nahi, ek hi niyam hai. Is niyam ko hi **Universal Law of Gravitation** kehte hain, jo hum agle topic mein padhenge.",
            "bodyHinglish": "**Kaha jaata hai** ki ek din Isaac Newton ek seb ke ped ke neeche baithe the, aur ek seb neeche gira. Is se Newton ke dimaag mein ek sawal aaya: \"Seb neeche kyun gira? Kya wahi force jo seb ko neeche kheench raha hai, chaand tak bhi pahunchta hai?\"\n\nYe kahani ek **legend (kimvadanti)** hai: kahani ka kitna hissa sach hai, ye pakka nahi kaha ja sakta. Par sachchi baat ye hai ki Newton ne ek bahut bada vichar socha: **jo force seb ko zameen par girata hai, wahi force chaand ko Dharti ke chakkar lagwata hai.** Yaani aasman aur zameen ke liye alag-alag niyam nahi, ek hi niyam hai. Is niyam ko hi **Universal Law of Gravitation** kehte hain, jo hum agle topic mein padhenge."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Part I: Gravity aur magnetic force ki tulna",
            "headingHinglish": "Part I: Gravity aur magnetic force ki tulna",
            "body": "Dono bina chhue kaam karte hain, par dono mein bhi farak hai.\n\n| Baat | Gravity | Magnetic force |\n|---|---|---|\n| Chhue bina kaam karta hai? | Haan, non-contact | Haan, non-contact |\n| Kheenchta hai? | Haan, hamesha | Haan (alag poles) |\n| Dhakelta hai? | Kabhi nahi | Haan (same poles) |\n| Kin cheezon par lagta hai? | **Har cheez jisme mass hai** | Sirf magnet aur kuch dhaatuon (jaise lohe) par |\n| Kab dikhta hai? | Jab ek cheez bahut badi ho (jaise Dharti) | Chhote magnet mein bhi dikh jata hai |",
            "bodyHinglish": "Dono bina chhue kaam karte hain, par dono mein bhi farak hai.\n\n| Baat | Gravity | Magnetic force |\n|---|---|---|\n| Chhue bina kaam karta hai? | Haan, non-contact | Haan, non-contact |\n| Kheenchta hai? | Haan, hamesha | Haan (alag poles) |\n| Dhakelta hai? | Kabhi nahi | Haan (same poles) |\n| Kin cheezon par lagta hai? | **Har cheez jisme mass hai** | Sirf magnet aur kuch dhaatuon (jaise lohe) par |\n| Kab dikhta hai? | Jab ek cheez bahut badi ho (jaise Dharti) | Chhote magnet mein bhi dikh jata hai |"
          },
          {
            "heading": "Aam galtiyan (bachke rehna)",
            "headingHinglish": "Aam galtiyan (bachke rehna)",
            "body": "**Galti 1: \"Gravity sirf zameen ke paas lagti hai.\"**\nSahi: gravity door tak jaati hai. Chaand ko Dharti ki gravity hi baandhe hue hai, jo lagbhag 3,84,000 km door hai. Doori badhne par ye kam hoti hai, par khatam nahi hoti.\n\n**Galti 2: \"Gravity hamesha neeche ki taraf hoti hai.\"**\nSahi: gravity hamesha **Dharti ke kendra ki taraf** hoti hai. Australia ke log hamse ulte lagte hain, par unke liye bhi \"neeche\" kendra ki taraf hi hai.\n\n**Galti 3: \"Antariksh mein gravity nahi hoti.\"**\nSahi: antariksh mein bhi gravity hoti hai. Isi ki wajah se chaand aur satellite Dharti ke paas bane rehte hain. Space station mein cheezein kyun tairti hain, ye Topic 5 aur 6 mein dekhenge.\n\n**Galti 4: \"Bhaari cheezein kheenchi jaati hain, halki nahi.\"**\nSahi: gravity har mass wali cheez par lagti hai, halki ho ya bhaari. Ek patta bhi Dharti ki taraf kheencha jaata hai, bas hawa ki wajah se dheere girta hai.\n\n**Galti 5: \"Aam Dharti ko nahi kheenchta, sirf Dharti aam ko kheenchti hai.\"**\nSahi: aam bhi Dharti ko kheenchta hai. Par Dharti ki mass bahut zyada hai, isliye uska asar na ke barabar hai. Ye \"kitna\" Topic 2 mein dekhenge.\n\n**Galti 6: \"Cheezein hawa ke dabav (air pressure) ki wajah se neeche tiki hain.\"**\nSahi: cheezein neeche gravity ki wajah se hain, hawa ke dabav se nahi. Hawa ka dabav kisi cheez ko neeche nahi kheenchta; ye alag cheez hai jise hum agle chapter (Pressure aur Fluids) mein dekhenge.",
            "bodyHinglish": "**Galti 1: \"Gravity sirf zameen ke paas lagti hai.\"**\nSahi: gravity door tak jaati hai. Chaand ko Dharti ki gravity hi baandhe hue hai, jo lagbhag 3,84,000 km door hai. Doori badhne par ye kam hoti hai, par khatam nahi hoti.\n\n**Galti 2: \"Gravity hamesha neeche ki taraf hoti hai.\"**\nSahi: gravity hamesha **Dharti ke kendra ki taraf** hoti hai. Australia ke log hamse ulte lagte hain, par unke liye bhi \"neeche\" kendra ki taraf hi hai.\n\n**Galti 3: \"Antariksh mein gravity nahi hoti.\"**\nSahi: antariksh mein bhi gravity hoti hai. Isi ki wajah se chaand aur satellite Dharti ke paas bane rehte hain. Space station mein cheezein kyun tairti hain, ye Topic 5 aur 6 mein dekhenge.\n\n**Galti 4: \"Bhaari cheezein kheenchi jaati hain, halki nahi.\"**\nSahi: gravity har mass wali cheez par lagti hai, halki ho ya bhaari. Ek patta bhi Dharti ki taraf kheencha jaata hai, bas hawa ki wajah se dheere girta hai.\n\n**Galti 5: \"Aam Dharti ko nahi kheenchta, sirf Dharti aam ko kheenchti hai.\"**\nSahi: aam bhi Dharti ko kheenchta hai. Par Dharti ki mass bahut zyada hai, isliye uska asar na ke barabar hai. Ye \"kitna\" Topic 2 mein dekhenge.\n\n**Galti 6: \"Cheezein hawa ke dabav (air pressure) ki wajah se neeche tiki hain.\"**\nSahi: cheezein neeche gravity ki wajah se hain, hawa ke dabav se nahi. Hawa ka dabav kisi cheez ko neeche nahi kheenchta; ye alag cheez hai jise hum agle chapter (Pressure aur Fluids) mein dekhenge."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Quick Recap",
            "headingHinglish": "Quick Recap",
            "body": "- Cheezein neeche isliye girti hain kyunki Dharti unhe kheench rahi hai: is kheenchav ko **gravity (gravitational force)** kehte hain.\n- Gravity kisi bhi do mass wali cheezon ke beech lagti hai: Dharti, Chaand, Sooraj, pen, kitaab, hum sab.\n- Dharti har cheez ko apne **kendra ki taraf** kheenchti hai, isliye Dharti ke har hisse mein \"neeche\" matlab kendra ki taraf.\n- Gravity hamesha **attractive** hai, kabhi repulsive nahi, aur ye **non-contact force** hai.\n- Pen aur kitaab ke beech gravity bahut chhoti hai: wo tab dikhti hai jab ek cheez bahut badi ho.\n- Gravity antariksh mein bhi hoti hai.\n- Newton ke laws gravity par bhi lagte hain: akela gravity lage to unbalanced force hai aur cheez accelerate karti hai; latke bulb mein gravity aur tension balanced hain.\n- Newton aur seb ki kahani ek legend hai; asli vichar ye hai ki seb aur chaand ko ek hi gravity chalati hai.",
            "bodyHinglish": "- Cheezein neeche isliye girti hain kyunki Dharti unhe kheench rahi hai: is kheenchav ko **gravity (gravitational force)** kehte hain.\n- Gravity kisi bhi do mass wali cheezon ke beech lagti hai: Dharti, Chaand, Sooraj, pen, kitaab, hum sab.\n- Dharti har cheez ko apne **kendra ki taraf** kheenchti hai, isliye Dharti ke har hisse mein \"neeche\" matlab kendra ki taraf.\n- Gravity hamesha **attractive** hai, kabhi repulsive nahi, aur ye **non-contact force** hai.\n- Pen aur kitaab ke beech gravity bahut chhoti hai: wo tab dikhti hai jab ek cheez bahut badi ho.\n- Gravity antariksh mein bhi hoti hai.\n- Newton ke laws gravity par bhi lagte hain: akela gravity lage to unbalanced force hai aur cheez accelerate karti hai; latke bulb mein gravity aur tension balanced hain.\n- Newton aur seb ki kahani ek legend hai; asli vichar ye hai ki seb aur chaand ko ek hi gravity chalati hai."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Practice Questions",
            "headingHinglish": "Practice Questions",
            "body": "**Q1.** State whether each statement is true or false.\n(a) Gravity acts only on heavy objects.\n(b) Gravity can push objects away from each other.\n(c) The Earth pulls objects towards its centre.\n(d) There is no gravity in outer space.\n(e) Gravity acts without touching.\n(f) A mango also pulls the Earth.\n\n**Q2.** Write the direction of the gravitational force in each situation.\n(a) A ball is thrown straight up and is at its highest point.\n(b) A ball is moving upwards just after being thrown.\n(c) A man is standing in Australia and drops a cup.\n(d) The Moon, as seen from the Earth.\n(e) A satellite orbiting the Earth.\n(f) A raindrop falling in Delhi.\n\n**Q3.** A lamp hangs at rest from a ceiling by a chain. Name the two forces acting on it, give their directions, and say whether they are balanced.\n\n**Q4.** Why do we not see a pen and a book on a table pulling each other?\n\n**Q5.** Gravity is called a non-contact force. Explain with one example. Name one other non-contact force.\n\n**Q6.** Why can people on the opposite side of the Earth also stand and walk without falling off?\n\n**Q7.** Using Newton's laws, explain why a mango accelerates downwards when it breaks off the tree, but stays still while it is attached.\n\n**Q8.** Give two differences between gravity and magnetic force.\n\n**Q9.** A 0.5 kg object has a net unbalanced force of 6 N acting on it. Find its acceleration. (This question is about F = ma, not gravity formulas.)\n\n**Q10.** A student says, \"Things stay on the ground because of air pressure pushing them down.\" Is this correct? Explain briefly.\n\n### Answers\n\n**A1.**\n(a) False ❌. Gravity acts on every object that has mass.\n(b) False ❌. Gravity is always attractive.\n(c) True ✅.\n(d) False ❌. Gravity exists in space too.\n(e) True ✅. It is a non-contact force.\n(f) True ✅. The effect on the Earth is just too tiny to notice.\n\n**A2.**\n(a) Downwards, towards the Earth's centre (gravity still acts at the highest point).\n(b) Downwards, towards the Earth's centre. The ball goes up because of the throw, but gravity still pulls it down.\n(c) Towards the Earth's centre. For the person in Australia, this is also \"down\".\n(d) The Earth pulls the Moon towards the Earth's centre, i.e. towards the Earth.\n(e) Towards the Earth's centre.\n(f) Downwards, towards the Earth's centre.\n\n**A3.** Gravity (weight) acts **downwards** and the tension in the chain acts **upwards**. They are **balanced** (equal and opposite), so the net force is zero and the lamp stays at rest.\n\n**A4.** Pen aur kitaab ke beech gravity hoti hai, par dono ki mass bahut kam hai, isliye kheenchav bahut hi chhota hai. Wo na dikhta hai, na pen ko hila sakta hai (table ka friction bhi rokta hai). Gravity tab dikhti hai jab ek cheez Dharti jaisi bahut badi ho.\n\n**A5.** Aam daal se toot kar bina kisi ke chhue Dharti ki taraf girta hai: Dharti ka gravity use door se kheench rahi hai. Ek aur non-contact force: magnetic force (ya electrostatic force).\n\n**A6.** Dharti har cheez ko apne kendra ki taraf kheenchti hai. Dusri taraf ke logon ke liye bhi \"neeche\" ka matlab kendra ki taraf hi hota hai, isliye wo bhi zameen par tike rehte hain.\n\n**A7.** Daal par laga aam rest mein hai kyunki daal ka upar ka force aur gravity ka neeche ka force balanced hain (Pehla Law). Jab wo toot jata hai, to daal ka force chala jata hai aur sirf gravity bachta hai: unbalanced force. Doosre Law (F = ma) ke hisaab se aam neeche ki taraf accelerate karta hai.\n\n**A8.** (i) Gravity hamesha attract karti hai; magnetic force attract aur repel dono kar sakta hai. (ii) Gravity har mass wali cheez par lagti hai; magnetic force sirf magnets aur kuch dhaatuon (jaise lohe) par.\n\n**A9.** a = F ÷ m = 6 N ÷ 0.5 kg = **12 m/s²**.\n**Jaanch:** 12 m/s² × 0.5 kg = 6 N ✅\n\n**A10.** Galat ❌. Cheezein gravity ki wajah se neeche rehti hain. Hawa ka dabav cheezon ko neeche nahi kheenchta, ye alag cheez hai.\n\n---\n\nEk hi gravity seb ko girati hai aur chaand ko Dharti ke aas-paas ghumati hai: ye jaan kar Hriday ki aankhein chamak gayi. Mr. Yadav ne aam uski taraf badhaya, \"Le, khaa. Gravity ne tere liye hi toda tha!\"\n\nAb sawal ye hai: **kitni zor se kheenchta hai?** Dharti aam ko kitne newton ke force se kheenchti hai, aur Chaand ko kitne se? Iska jawab Newton ke ek famous formula mein chhupa hai, jo hum **Topic 2** mein seekhenge.",
            "bodyHinglish": "**Q1.** State whether each statement is true or false.\n(a) Gravity acts only on heavy objects.\n(b) Gravity can push objects away from each other.\n(c) The Earth pulls objects towards its centre.\n(d) There is no gravity in outer space.\n(e) Gravity acts without touching.\n(f) A mango also pulls the Earth.\n\n**Q2.** Write the direction of the gravitational force in each situation.\n(a) A ball is thrown straight up and is at its highest point.\n(b) A ball is moving upwards just after being thrown.\n(c) A man is standing in Australia and drops a cup.\n(d) The Moon, as seen from the Earth.\n(e) A satellite orbiting the Earth.\n(f) A raindrop falling in Delhi.\n\n**Q3.** A lamp hangs at rest from a ceiling by a chain. Name the two forces acting on it, give their directions, and say whether they are balanced.\n\n**Q4.** Why do we not see a pen and a book on a table pulling each other?\n\n**Q5.** Gravity is called a non-contact force. Explain with one example. Name one other non-contact force.\n\n**Q6.** Why can people on the opposite side of the Earth also stand and walk without falling off?\n\n**Q7.** Using Newton's laws, explain why a mango accelerates downwards when it breaks off the tree, but stays still while it is attached.\n\n**Q8.** Give two differences between gravity and magnetic force.\n\n**Q9.** A 0.5 kg object has a net unbalanced force of 6 N acting on it. Find its acceleration. (This question is about F = ma, not gravity formulas.)\n\n**Q10.** A student says, \"Things stay on the ground because of air pressure pushing them down.\" Is this correct? Explain briefly.\n\n### Answers\n\n**A1.**\n(a) False ❌. Gravity acts on every object that has mass.\n(b) False ❌. Gravity is always attractive.\n(c) True ✅.\n(d) False ❌. Gravity exists in space too.\n(e) True ✅. It is a non-contact force.\n(f) True ✅. The effect on the Earth is just too tiny to notice.\n\n**A2.**\n(a) Downwards, towards the Earth's centre (gravity still acts at the highest point).\n(b) Downwards, towards the Earth's centre. The ball goes up because of the throw, but gravity still pulls it down.\n(c) Towards the Earth's centre. For the person in Australia, this is also \"down\".\n(d) The Earth pulls the Moon towards the Earth's centre, i.e. towards the Earth.\n(e) Towards the Earth's centre.\n(f) Downwards, towards the Earth's centre.\n\n**A3.** Gravity (weight) acts **downwards** and the tension in the chain acts **upwards**. They are **balanced** (equal and opposite), so the net force is zero and the lamp stays at rest.\n\n**A4.** Pen aur kitaab ke beech gravity hoti hai, par dono ki mass bahut kam hai, isliye kheenchav bahut hi chhota hai. Wo na dikhta hai, na pen ko hila sakta hai (table ka friction bhi rokta hai). Gravity tab dikhti hai jab ek cheez Dharti jaisi bahut badi ho.\n\n**A5.** Aam daal se toot kar bina kisi ke chhue Dharti ki taraf girta hai: Dharti ka gravity use door se kheench rahi hai. Ek aur non-contact force: magnetic force (ya electrostatic force).\n\n**A6.** Dharti har cheez ko apne kendra ki taraf kheenchti hai. Dusri taraf ke logon ke liye bhi \"neeche\" ka matlab kendra ki taraf hi hota hai, isliye wo bhi zameen par tike rehte hain.\n\n**A7.** Daal par laga aam rest mein hai kyunki daal ka upar ka force aur gravity ka neeche ka force balanced hain (Pehla Law). Jab wo toot jata hai, to daal ka force chala jata hai aur sirf gravity bachta hai: unbalanced force. Doosre Law (F = ma) ke hisaab se aam neeche ki taraf accelerate karta hai.\n\n**A8.** (i) Gravity hamesha attract karti hai; magnetic force attract aur repel dono kar sakta hai. (ii) Gravity har mass wali cheez par lagti hai; magnetic force sirf magnets aur kuch dhaatuon (jaise lohe) par.\n\n**A9.** a = F ÷ m = 6 N ÷ 0.5 kg = **12 m/s²**.\n**Jaanch:** 12 m/s² × 0.5 kg = 6 N ✅\n\n**A10.** Galat ❌. Cheezein gravity ki wajah se neeche rehti hain. Hawa ka dabav cheezon ko neeche nahi kheenchta, ye alag cheez hai.\n\n---\n\nEk hi gravity seb ko girati hai aur chaand ko Dharti ke aas-paas ghumati hai: ye jaan kar Hriday ki aankhein chamak gayi. Mr. Yadav ne aam uski taraf badhaya, \"Le, khaa. Gravity ne tere liye hi toda tha!\"\n\nAb sawal ye hai: **kitni zor se kheenchta hai?** Dharti aam ko kitne newton ke force se kheenchti hai, aur Chaand ko kitne se? Iska jawab Newton ke ek famous formula mein chhupa hai, jo hum **Topic 2** mein seekhenge."
          }
